 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Shifting",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each rising edge on SHCP takes the level on DS into stage 0 and moves every stage up by one. Stage 7 is brought out on Q7S, which can feed DS of a further device to make longer registers."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Latching and outputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A rising edge on STCP copies all eight shift stages into the storage register, whose contents drive Q0 to Q7 while OE is LOW. With OE HIGH the parallel outputs float; OE changes neither register. Q7S is not affected by OE."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Tied clocks",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "When SHCP and STCP share one clock, the storage register always holds the data one clock behind the shift register."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reset",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "MR LOW clears the shift register at once, without a clock; the storage register keeps its data until the next STCP edge. After MR returns HIGH, trec must pass before the next SHCP edge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input levels and mixed supplies",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "74AHC595 inputs switch at CMOS levels and 74AHCT595 inputs at TTL levels. All inputs of both carry Schmitt-trigger action and accept up to 5.5 V whatever the supply, so the parts can translate levels between systems on different supplies."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Applications",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Serial-to-parallel conversion; holding register for remote control."
  }
 ],
